          { name: "Dismissing Renter or Younger-Adult Concerns as Special Interests or Anti-Business Positions", tag: "avoid", why: "Any candidate whose framing treats housing affordability, healthcare cost, or workforce development as anti-business positions rather than mainstream constituent concerns will find opposition effectively mobilized among the renter and younger-adult constituencies that currently underperform in turnout. In Minnesota's political environment, these are not fringe issues — they are mainstream quality-of-life concerns that swing voters evaluate on pragmatic grounds. A representative who positions themselves as dismissive of these concerns is creating an opening for a challenger to make a credible case that the incumbent is indifferent to non-homeowner constituencies, which can shift both turnout and persuasion dynamics in a district this close to being competitive." }
