          "Engage younger adults' economic concerns explicitly: the 46.6-year median age in a homeowner-heavy district masks the reality that roughly 30-40% of households likely include younger earners or adult children facing different economic constraints than their parents' generation — healthcare costs, education debt, and wage stagnation are not abstract issues for this cohort.",
          "Recognize the 36% opposition as a mobilizable coalition rather than a fixed Democratic base: the Democratic vote is not monolithic, and neither is the Republican vote; a challenger who activates renter turnout and focuses on economic mobility could credibly contest this seat if the incumbent is perceived as governing primarily for homeowners.",
          "Avoid any messaging that treats homeownership as a prerequisite for full constituent status or that frames renter concerns as special interests competing with 'real' community concerns: renters are constituents with legitimate material interests, and any candidate whose rhetoric or policy priorities systematically devalue renter households will find opposition mobilized on that basis."
