          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Voters & Education Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 29.1% college attainment in a $84,679-income district, a meaningful proportion of SD-20 voters are college-educated professionals and families for whom education quality, K-12 system investment, and higher education access are primary decision factors. Nationally, college-educated voters in suburban districts have shifted toward Democratic candidates over recent cycles, especially on education and institutional governance. In SD-20, the 62-38 result suggests the current Republican margin does not necessarily reflect secure alignment with this cohort; it reflects consolidation of the non-college homeowner base. Any candidate must actively cultivate college-educated suburban voters with credible education investment messaging." },
          { name: "Suburban Healthcare Access & Institutional Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $84,679 income and 42.7 median age mean SD-20 voters are employed professionals and aging homeowners for whom healthcare quality, access to specialists, and insurance stability are primary quality-of-life concerns. Healthcare outcomes, rural hospital viability in exurban areas of the district, and insurance marketplace function are direct constituent concerns where candidates are evaluated on competence and results rather than ideology. A candidate who demonstrates fluency on healthcare systems and access issues builds credibility with the professional cohort that holds swing potential." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity in a 91% White District", tag: "careful", why: "The ~3.6% gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents Hispanic, Asian, Black, and AIAN residents whose specific concerns can become invisible in a district where the dominant story is the prosperous white homeowner base. While this cohort is too small to swing the district unilaterally, its treatment reflects broader candidate credibility on inclusion and demographic change. In suburban districts, how a candidate addresses growing diversity—whether with defensiveness or genuine welcome—signals broader institutional openness to the college-educated professional voters who tend to evaluate on this dimension." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as a Durable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result in a suburban district with 29.1% college attainment and high income is not inherently durable—it reflects current alignment but not necessarily secure voter commitment. College-educated suburban voters have demonstrated significant volatility across recent election cycles, and any candidate who assumes the 62% is a floor without actively holding the college-educated professional cohort is neglecting the swing voters who determine whether the margin expands or contracts in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Positioning Education and Healthcare as Zero-Sum Budget Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In suburban districts where the primary swing cohort is college-educated professionals with children and aging parents, framing education and healthcare as spending problems rather than investment opportunities in community quality cedes credibility with the voters most likely to shift between parties. A candidate who defaults to tax-cutting messaging without addressing education and healthcare outcomes directly is conceding the professional constituency that the 29.1% college attainment represents and that national suburban trends suggest is increasingly available." }
