          { name: "Housing Security and Rental Market Regulation", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 36.3% of households renting, housing costs and tenant protections are direct household budget concerns for more than one in three constituents in SD-39. Rising rents, eviction risk, and long-term rental instability are the material economic reality for a substantial portion of the district's working-class and professional-class residents. Legislation addressing rent stabilization, tenant protections, affordable housing development, and speculation in the rental market is not peripheral to a renter-majority district — it is core constituent service. A candidate who demonstrates concrete progress on housing access and rental security commands credibility with the constituency most directly affected by these issues." },
          { name: "Economic Opportunity and Cost-of-Living Relief for Working-Class Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $78,534 median income with 36.3% renters and 37.2% college attainment, SD-39's constituency is working-to-professional-class and evaluating whether government policy is measurably improving their economic security. Wage growth, childcare costs, healthcare affordability, and education funding directly impact household finances for residents at this income level. A legislative agenda that addresses wage floors, benefits access, and the cost drivers that constrain economic mobility for working families demonstrates responsiveness to the constituency's actual economic concerns rather than abstract partisan positioning." },
          { name: "Racial Equity and Community Investment in Majority-Minority Neighborhoods", tag: "careful", why: "The 18.11% Black and 12.35% Hispanic population together represent 30.46% of the district — more than one in four residents — with specific civic and economic concerns that demand active constituent attention beyond symbolic acknowledgment. School funding equity, criminal justice reform, workforce development investment, and economic development in majority-minority neighborhoods are the issues that determine whether these communities perceive the incumbent as actively serving their interests or simply counting on Democratic loyalty. Their turnout, candidate preference, and perception of measurable progress on these fronts are swing factors in a district where 67% is a current margin, not a stable floor." },
          { name: "Treating 67% as a Safe Supermajority That Requires No Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% result in a district with 36.3% renters, 30.46% combined Black and Hispanic population, and a working-to-professional-class constituency that is actively evaluating economic outcomes is not a floor — it is contingent on the incumbent remaining visibly responsive to each constituency segment's specific concerns. The margin reflects current alignment, not structural loyalty. Any candidate who assumes that partisan identity is sufficient to hold SD-39 without demonstrating measurable progress on housing security, economic opportunity, and racial equity will find the margin eroding in the face of a credible challenger offering concrete solutions on these issues." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Specific Economic Concerns of the 36.3% Renter Population", tag: "avoid", why: "More than one in three households in SD-39 do not own property, meaning housing costs, eviction risk, and rental market conditions are not abstract policy concerns but direct household budget realities. A candidate who fails to prioritize concrete housing solutions — tenant protections, rent stabilization, affordable housing development — is signaling that they do not understand or do not prioritize the material economic concerns of a substantial and growing constituency segment. In a competitive political environment, this inattention creates an opening for a challenger who offers specific housing-focused solutions." }
