          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost Control", tag: "lean-into", why: "Affluent suburban professionals earning $102,821 median income are still vulnerable to healthcare cost shocks and insurance premium volatility. Drug pricing, insurance coverage adequacy, and mental health access are the specific healthcare concerns most likely to activate this constituency's vote. A candidate who offers concrete policy positions on prescription drug cost control and insurance reform directly addresses material household budget concerns for a constituency that otherwise has stable economic standing." },
          { name: "Professional-Class Economic Interests and Tax Policy", tag: "careful", why: "The 65% Democratic margin in an affluent, college-educated suburban district rests partly on perception of Democratic competence and institutional quality, but also on balance-of-interest calculations about taxes, government efficiency, and whether government spending delivers returns. With 40.4 median age and high homeownership, this is a constituency sensitive to property tax burdens and state income tax fairness. A candidate who dismisses or minimizes tax burden concerns in favor of abstract appeals to government spending risks appearing to neglect the material interests of voters who are voting Democratic not on ideology but on perceived performance." },
          { name: "Tribal Nations or Native American Swing Dynamics", tag: "avoid", why: "With 73.37% white, 8.66% Asian, 6.28% Black, and 6.04% Hispanic populations, and no demographic gap suggesting significant Native American population, MN SD-40 does not have the tribal or Native American governance dynamics that structure elections in other districts. Any candidate who frames this election around tribal partnerships or Native American community engagement is misreading the district's actual constituent base and wasting strategic resources on a non-existent swing constituency." },
          { name: "Treating 65% as a Durable Floor Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 65-35 margin in a district that realigned toward Democrats on competence and professional-class interest grounds is not immutable — it reflects specific alignment between the incumbent's positioning and what a college-educated, affluent suburban constituency currently prioritizes. Any candidate who assumes this margin will hold without actively addressing education funding, healthcare costs, and tax fairness is making a dangerous assumption. In a presidential midterm or against a well-resourced challenger, this margin can compress to 58-60% if the professional-class base concludes they are not being heard on their material concerns." }
