          { name: "College-Educated Constituency Accountability on Implementation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 69.3% college-educated majority describes voters capable of sophisticated policy analysis and evaluation of implementation outcomes, not voters susceptible to partisan rhetoric alone. This constituency expects specific, measurable results on the issues they prioritize. A legislator who cannot explain actual legislative achievements, budget trade-offs, and why specific policies work or fail will lose credibility with voters who evaluate on substance rather than ideology." },
          { name: "Younger-Adult Pragmatism Over Inherited Partisan Loyalty", tag: "careful", why: "At 35.5 median age with a 58.8% renter rate, SD-61 contains a constituency that is politically pragmatic and economically anxious rather than ideologically inherited. If housing costs continue to rise, if transit fails to improve, or if this constituency concludes that government is not prioritizing their material concerns, the absence of an alternative candidate masks vulnerability — not strength. Any incumbent must actively maintain constituent connection with younger adults, not assume the 100% result reflects durable loyalty." },
          { name: "Running on Social Issues Without Addressing Economic Anxiety", tag: "avoid", why: "A college-educated, urban, younger-adult constituency that is 58.8% renters will not be mobilized primarily by social-issue positioning divorced from economic material concerns. Campaigning on abstract progressive values while ignoring housing affordability, living costs, and whether young adults can afford to remain in the district mistakes what this constituency actually cares about and creates vulnerability to a challenger who leads with economic security." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed 100% as Durable Democratic Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result in the absence of opposition reflects only that no alternative candidate emerged, not that the district is safely Democratic or that the incumbent has built an unshakeable coalition. The structural profile — young, renting, college-educated, economically anxious in a high-cost-of-living market — is actually quite vulnerable to a challenger who can credibly promise better economic outcomes. Operating under the assumption that 100% is a floor rather than a temporary artifact of no opposition is a dangerous strategic miscalculation." }
