          { name: "Diverse Community Economic Opportunity & Racial Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.64% Black, 11.91% Hispanic, and 13.5% Asian communities together represent 46.05% of the district—nearly half—with distinct economic and social concerns that require specific policy solutions rather than generic minority-community messaging. Workforce development and employment discrimination remedies for Black residents, immigration protection and workplace rights for Hispanic residents, and language access and healthcare equity for Asian residents are not separate campaigns but the infrastructure of genuine constituent service in a plural-majority district. A representative who consolidates only white and college-educated voters while treating other communities as reliable base votes will find the coalition fracturing as younger adults and renters conclude they are not being served." },
          { name: "Young Adult Constituency & Educational Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 33.7-year median age and 35.9% college attainment reflect a younger district where educational pathways and the transition from education to economic stability are material governance concerns. The college-attainment rate is below many urban Democratic strongholds, suggesting a constituency with substantial numbers of high-school-educated and vocational-pathway workers for whom training access, wage growth, and workplace protections are the primary economic mobility levers. A representative who focuses legislative energy primarily on four-year university pathways while neglecting vocational training and workforce development risks losing younger adults who see the system as designed for someone else's children." },
          { name: "Treating 80% as a Safe Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80-20 result in a younger, renter-majority, economically diverse district with substantial numbers of voters evaluating on material delivery rather than inherited party loyalty is not a safe floor—it is an actively maintained coalition that erodes if the incumbent treats the margin as permission to govern without constituent engagement. Complacency about housing policy, racial equity service, or economic development for younger adults will shrink the margin in the next competitive cycle; the 80% is real but requires continuous active defense." },
          { name: "Positioning the District as 'Post-Racial' or Unified Without Specific Racial Equity Work", tag: "avoid", why: "In a plural-majority district where no single racial or ethnic group commands 50%, generic appeals to 'unity' or 'serving everyone equally' without specific, differentiated attention to each community's distinct economic and social concerns signal to those communities that their particular needs are not being taken seriously. The 46.28% white population is large enough to make race-blind positioning seem plausible, but doing so writes off nearly three-quarters of the district's communities of color and will erode the coalition as those communities conclude they are being asked to support a representative who does not actually serve them." }
