          { name: "Aging Homeowner Healthcare Access & Cost", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47.1-year median age combined with 82.4% homeownership rate signals a constituency that is aging in place in a rural region with declining healthcare infrastructure. Rural hospital closures, Medicare access, prescription drug costs, and long-term care availability are not abstract policy concerns but immediate household budget realities for constituents managing healthcare costs on $61,466 median income. A representative who can articulate specific legislative solutions to rural healthcare access — not just acknowledgment — and who demonstrates consistent advocacy for rural hospital viability and Medicare sustainability commands credibility that generic conservative rhetoric does not." },
          { name: "Property Tax Burden & Local Government Finance", tag: "careful", why: "Property tax management is a direct household budget issue for an 82.4% homeowner constituency on median $61,466 income, and rural Minnesota property values and tax rates vary significantly by county and township. Without understanding the specific property tax structure, assessment practices, and local revenue pressures in this particular district, a representative risks appearing disconnected from the material concerns that drive homeowner frustration — particularly in regions where agricultural property is reassessed at higher rates or where consolidated school districts have raised operational costs. Specific knowledge of the local property tax environment and credible legislative proposals for fairness matter more than generic tax-cut rhetoric." },
          { name: "Treating 60-40 as a Partisan Mandate Rather Than Incumbent Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 Republican margin in a 91.59% white rural district should produce a 70-30 or higher result if pure demographics determined outcome; the 40% opposition in a demographically homogeneous constituency signals that incumbent-specific satisfaction or dissatisfaction, not inherited partisan loyalty, is the driving force. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — who campaigns this seat as though 60% represents a durable coalition rather than a conditional endorsement based on perceived economic performance will miss the actual vulnerability: if constituents believe their representative is ineffective on rural economic sustainability, healthcare access, or property tax fairness, the 40% can grow quickly." },
          { name: "Assuming Low Education Attainment Correlates With Low Policy Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20.1% college-educated rate in this district reflects limited higher-education infrastructure and professional-class employment, not constituent sophistication about agricultural policy, water rights, property tax law, or healthcare finance. Rural homeowners and farmers often have deep technical knowledge of agricultural economics, water management, land valuation, and healthcare systems — and they evaluate legislative performance on whether their representative understands these domains with specificity. A representative who speaks generically about 'supporting farmers' or 'protecting healthcare' without demonstrating concrete knowledge of agricultural input markets, water policy, or rural hospital finance will be perceived as disconnected regardless of partisan alignment, and this perception is what creates vulnerability in a 60-40 seat." }
