          { name: "Treating 72% as Indicative of Deep Structural Support", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72% margin in a district with an aging population (48.3 median age), modest income ($62,641), and a 7% unaccounted demographic gap does not represent deep structural attachment — it reflects current consolidation of the older white homeowner base. National midterm swings, demographic shifts, or effective challenger outreach to renters and the AIAN community could move this margin 5-8 points. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 72% is a stable foundation rather than a coalition requiring active maintenance will be vulnerable when circumstances tighten." },
          { name: "Assuming Renters Are Immobilized or Unimportant", tag: "avoid", why: "At 17.8% renter rate, HD-10A has a meaningful population of households without homeowner equity — a group in older, working-class districts that often includes both younger families priced out of ownership and older adults on fixed incomes. Housing affordability, rental regulations, and the economic security concerns of non-homeowners are treated as peripheral issues at the representative's peril. A challenger who makes visible overtures to this constituency while the incumbent ignores it creates an opening that is disproportionate to the group's absolute size." }
