          { name: "Homeowner Property Value & Tax Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 24.3% renter rate combined with $71,660 median household income and 40.4 median age reflects a majority-homeowner constituency for whom property values, tax fairness, and residential stability are primary political concerns. This is the core of the 72% Republican vote—homeowners whose material stake in property appreciation and tax policy naturally aligns with Republican fiscal positioning. Messaging that emphasizes tax discipline, property value protection, and neighborhood stability directly reinforces Republican consolidation of this base." },
          { name: "Education Quality & School District Performance", tag: "careful", why: "The 25.1% college attainment combined with 40.4 median age suggests a substantial population of college-educated parents with school-age children whose political choices are influenced by school district quality and education opportunity. The 28% Democratic opposition in a district this advantaged likely includes parents and professionals who believe current education funding or governance frameworks are insufficient for maintaining competitive schools. Any incumbent or challenger who fails to engage this constituency on specific, measurable commitments to education quality risks losing 2-3 percentage points among this concentrated, high-engagement voter segment." },
          { name: "Partisan Cultural Warfare as a Base Consolidation Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 28% of educated, propertied homeowners are already voting Democratic despite demographic pull toward Republicans, and where another subset of the Republican base is likely motivated primarily by competence and institutional performance rather than cultural grievance, messaging centered on partisan cultural conflict risks alienating the exact swing voters most essential to either defending or expanding the current margin. Heavy reliance on cultural warfare framing is more likely to shift this district toward 65-35 than to protect the current 72." },
          { name: "Treating Minority Communities as Peripheral or Symbolic", tag: "avoid", why: "While the Hispanic (6.18%) and other minority communities are individually too small to determine the district outcome, their aggregate share (approximately 10-11% of the population) is substantive enough that gains or losses of 1-2 points each can shift the overall margin by 2-3 points—the difference between a 72% win and a 69-70% win. Any incumbent or challenger who treats these communities as peripheral constituencies rather than engaging them on material economic and healthcare concerns risks both alienating voters who might otherwise be persuadable and signaling to the broader college-educated professional constituency that institutional inclusion and respect are not governing priorities." }
