          "Lead with homeowner economic security — property tax fairness, healthcare cost management, and rural service viability are the direct governance concerns for a 79% homeowning constituency at $70,542 median income with a 41.9-year median age; these are the issues most likely to generate persuadable opposition if left unaddressed.",
          "Address the college attainment gap with specificity: 18.9% college-educated adults signals limited local higher education access or workforce pipeline — youth retention, vocational training, and the state-level investment in rural education infrastructure are the real economic development levers for a district with this profile.",
          "Engage the 2.91% demographic gap — most likely American Indian / Alaska Native residents in rural Minnesota — with specific constituent attention rather than token acknowledgment; rural tribal communities have direct needs around economic development, healthcare access, and state-tribal coordination that deserve active legislative engagement.",
          "Avoid treating 72% as a landslide floor: a 72-28 margin in a homeowner-heavy, middle-income rural district is durable only if the incumbent actively maintains constituent relationships and delivers on the material concerns that drive voting behavior in this income and age cohort; passive incumbency in a district this competitive by national standards is a strategic error."
