          { name: "Treating 42% Opposition as Ignorable", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with clear Republican structural advantage (90% white, 77.6% homeowners, $76,951 income, 26.8% college education), a 42% Democratic result should trigger diagnostic analysis rather than confident assumption that the margin will expand automatically. The 42% is not noise—it represents real voters, many of them homeowners and college-educated, who actively preferred Democratic alignment. A candidate who dismisses this 42% as permanent opposition rather than understanding what drove it and addressing it directly risks allowing that opposition to organize and mobilize more effectively in a future cycle." },
          { name: "Immigration Enforcement as a Mobilizing Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "Although the Hispanic population at 3.69% is not yet a dominant electoral force in MN-HD-20A, any aggressive positioning on immigration enforcement or family separation risks activating this community politically and signaling to Hispanic-owned businesses and families that the incumbent does not view them as constituent stakeholders. In a district where maintaining a 16-point margin depends on not losing ground among college-educated swing voters who may value inclusive governance, aggressive immigration rhetoric is strategically counterproductive and exposes the seat to Democratic challenger activation of both Hispanic voters and college-educated homeowners concerned about family separation and civil rights." }
