          { name: "School Quality & Education Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median age of 36 and 53% college attainment concentrated in a professional-class suburb, K-12 school quality and higher education affordability are material household concerns, not abstract platform items. Parents in this district actively evaluate candidates on whether they can demonstrate measurable results on school outcomes, funding mechanisms, and whether educational opportunity for their children is expanding or contracting. Democratic positioning that takes school quality seriously as a governance priority — with specific outcomes — is necessary to maintain alignment with a constituency that will switch votes if they perceive Democratic stewardship as producing worse schools." },
          { name: "Perceived Competence on Economic Stewardship", tag: "careful", why: "A 56-44 margin among affluent professionals means that a meaningful 44% of the district is willing to vote Republican, suggesting that Republican economic messaging — property tax fairness, fiscal responsibility, efficient government — resonates with a real portion of the constituent base. The Democratic incumbent must continuously defend against framing that positions Republican governance as better for high-income households' economic interests. This is not an automatic Democratic constituency; it is a persuadable one that evaluates on whether government is delivering competently for their material interests — and Republican candidates who effectively argue that Democratic governance is inefficient or threatening to wealth preservation can erode the margin." },
          { name: "National Partisan Polarization & Local Seat Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "While the district is 56-44 Democratic in 2024, the margin reflects a specific moment in national partisan alignment and a particular incumbent profile. The assumption that 56% in a presidential cycle predicts 56% in a midterm or special election is false — professional-class suburban voters are more volatile than their apparent partisan lean suggests, and turnout fluctuations of 10-15 percentage points between election types are normal. Treating this as a safe seat or assuming that the 44% opposition is a fixed cap on Republican potential is strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Demographics as Electoral Destiny", tag: "avoid", why: "The 71% white, 11.7% Black, 8.24% Asian composition and the high income and education levels might suggest a naturally Democratic constituency, but the actual vote is 56-44, not 65-35 or 70-30. The assumption that demographic diversity or education level automatically produces large Democratic margins ignores the material reality that this constituency votes on economic competence and alignment with their interests, not on demographic category. A candidate who campaigns this seat as if it should be safely Democratic based on demographics rather than on continuous engagement with voters' material concerns will find the margin shrinking." }
