          { name: "Housing Affordability and Development Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 42.6% renter rate and $73,930 median income mean housing costs are a material economic concern for a substantial portion of the district. Zoning reform, support for multifamily development, transit-oriented housing, and protections against displacement are policy levers with direct impact on whether young professionals and working families can remain in the district. A Democratic incumbent who can show concrete progress on housing supply and affordability directly addresses the economic anxiety that might otherwise create an opening for a Republican challenger focused on cost of living." },
          { name: "Fiscal Discipline and Regulatory Pragmatism", tag: "careful", why: "The 36% Republican minority in a 46.1% college-educated district suggests that Republican voters are not primarily motivated by cultural conservatism but by concerns about taxes, regulatory burden, and government efficiency. At $73,930 median income, even professional-class households care about property tax fairness and the cost of government. An incumbent who ignores the Republican constituency's fiscal concerns or dismisses them as ideological rather than practical risks allowing a credible challenger to expand their base by positioning as pragmatic stewards rather than partisan opponents." },
          { name: "Anti-Government or Anti-Tax Ideological Appeals", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 36% Republican minority deserves engagement on fiscal concerns, appeals based on the ideology of small government or blanket tax reduction are misaligned with a 64% Democratic district where college-educated professionals understand trade-offs between public investment and service quality. An incumbent who campaigns against government itself or promises tax cuts without addressing what they would cut is speaking to a minority of the district and risking loss of credibility with the professional, service-valuing majority." },
          { name: "Cultural or Identity Politics Divisive Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 46.1% college attainment and meaningful racial and ethnic diversity (70.69% white, but with 9.57% Black, 7.25% Hispanic, 6.83% Asian populations) evaluates on whether government is delivering services and opportunity, not on cultural grievance or identity-based positioning. An incumbent who campaigns through cultural divisiveness rather than shared service delivery risks fragmenting the coalition that produced the 64% margin and energizing Republican opposition around themes that actually do motivate their base. This district's 64% is built on a positive vision of diverse, educated community, not on anti-conservative sentiment." }
