          "Lead with housing affordability and renter economic mobility: the 33.9% renter rate at $65,865 income signals a constituency experiencing genuine housing cost pressure; messaging that treats renters as a permanent underclass rather than as constituents building toward economic stability will underperform; the winning message connects housing policy, childcare costs, and wage growth to a narrative about economic mobility and building wealth in HD-26A.",
          "Treat the 47% opposition as the primary strategic fact: in a district this narrowly held, the opposition is not a small, mobilizable fringe — it is nearly half the constituency, and identifying whether it is concentrated among college-educated voters frustrated with healthcare or education policy, among renters squeezed by housing costs, or both, and offering concrete answers to each group, is the single most important strategic work any candidate can do.",
          "Avoid positioning this as a safely Republican district: a 53-47 margin in a suburban Minnesota district with 32% college attainment and substantial renter population is not a stable majority — it is a competitive seat that will shift if the incumbent fails to maintain active engagement with the college-educated and renter constituencies or if a challenger effectively mobilizes them around education, healthcare, or housing affordability."
