          { name: "Property Tax and Land-Use Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 14.2% renter rate and $100,228 median household income, HD-27B is a heavily owner-occupied community with substantial property equity and direct financial stakes in property tax levels and local zoning decisions. Property tax fairness, residential zoning protection, and agricultural land preservation are the governance issues that matter most to this homeowner constituency. A legislative record that addresses property tax burden and protects residential and agricultural land values from unwanted development or regulatory change is essential to maintaining the 74% consolidation." },
          { name: "American Indian and Alaska Native Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~5.6% AIAN gap in the demographic data represents 840-900 residents with direct stakes in tribal governance, Indian Health Service access, cultural resource protection, and the state-tribal relationship — issues that affect AIAN constituents differently than the general population. While this community is smaller in absolute terms than in heavily tribal-adjacent districts, it is large enough to constitute a meaningful constituency with specific needs that deserve direct engagement. Any legislator who assumes AIAN constituents are fully represented in the general population, rather than actively engaging them on their specific concerns, risks both a governance shortfall and latent opposition in future cycles." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Permanently Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "While 74% reflects strong consolidation of the homeowner and small-business base, it is not a bulletproof result in Minnesota's competitive political environment. The renter population (14.2%), younger adults, and AIAN constituents represent groups whose needs may not be fully reflected in the current margin. Any incumbent who campaigns on the assumption that 74% is a stable floor, rather than actively consolidating the existing base and preventing opposition from mobilizing among underserved constituencies, creates an opening for a challenger with more targeted engagement." },
          { name: "Educational Divide and Professional-Class Alienation", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20.1% college attainment rate in Minnesota — where the statewide rate is substantially higher and many surrounding districts have 30%+ college-educated populations — signals a genuine cultural and economic division between HD-27B and the state's professional-class base. Any candidate who frames the district's interests in opposition to higher education, professional expertise, or the college-educated workforce, or who campaigns in ways that deepen this cultural divide, risks reducing the seat's durability and making it more vulnerable to challenger mobilization if the partisan environment shifts. Consolidation of the existing base does not require cultural antagonism toward constituencies outside the district." }
