          "Engage multiethnic communities on concrete economic outcomes: with 43.3% of the district non-white and median income only $78,153, the 64% margin is held by younger, working-class voters of color whose commitment to any party is contingent on whether they perceive government delivering on jobs, housing, and equitable opportunity—abstract messaging will not hold this coalition against a credible challenger offering specific economic remedies.",
          "Address the college-attainment gap: at 29.5% college-educated, the district has a substantial population of high-school and some-college-educated workers in a metro economy increasingly stratified by educational credential; workforce development, apprenticeship access, and pathways to economic stability without a four-year degree are the retention issue for a significant portion of the constituency.",
          "Do not assume renter invisibility in housing policy: renters at 34.3% of the district are often treated as a secondary constituency in state-level housing discussions; a candidate who treats renter protections, affordable housing development, and renter-specific cost pressures as primary rather than secondary is addressing the material concerns of more than one-third of the district."
