          { name: "Communities of Color Economic Mobility & Service Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.03% Black population plus 5.67% Hispanic, 3.37% Asian, and roughly 8-10% American Indian and Alaska Native communities together represent approaching one-third of HD-43A. These communities experience disproportionate impacts from housing insecurity, healthcare access constraints, and policing and economic opportunity barriers. An incumbent who engages these constituencies with specific constituent service, policy responsiveness, and authentic partnership is building the relationships that sustain the 66% margin; an incumbent who treats communities of color as a voting bloc rather than a coalition of distinct communities with specific needs risks losing their active participation and opening the 66% to erosion." },
          { name: "Understanding the 34% Opposition in an Urban Democratic District", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this Democratic, the 34% Republican and opposition vote likely does not reflect the constituency's overall partisan distribution but rather concentrates among specific demographics or represents swing voters frustrated by specific incumbency failures. The opposition might include renters frustrated by housing policy, communities of color without responsive service, younger adults concerned about economic opportunity, or suburban-oriented voters who feel the district's urban focus neglects them. Identifying specifically where the 34% comes from and what drove them away is crucial intelligence for understanding what maintenance the 66% actually requires." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Structural Supermajority Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result in a district with 36.5% renters, 20%+ Black population, substantial other communities of color, and economic diversity is not a floor — it is a maintained coalition of renters, communities of color, and younger adults whose concerns require active incumbent attention. The moment an incumbent campaigns as though 66% is permission to govern without engaging renters on housing, communities of color on service and equity, or younger adults on economic mobility, the margin begins to erode. Supermajority complacency is the primary risk to this seat." },
          { name: "Messaging That Centers the Incumbent's Achievements Over Constituent Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where renters are experiencing housing affordability pressure, communities of color face systemic barriers to economic mobility and service access, and the median age of 39.5 reflects substantial younger-adult presence, a campaign that emphasizes incumbent legislative accomplishments or abstract party messaging rather than material responsiveness to renter concerns, community-specific service, and economic opportunity will fail to maintain coalition activation. Constituents in HD-43A evaluate on whether government is materially improving their housing stability, their access to services, and their economic opportunity — not on incumbent seniority or partisan positioning." }
