          "Engage the Black community, Hispanic community, and other communities of color with specific constituent service and policy attention: combined, these communities represent approaching one-third of the district, and their economic mobility, healthcare access, and policing concerns are material governance priorities, not symbolic acknowledgments.",
          "Treat the 34% opposition as information rather than noise: in a district this Democratic, the opposition likely concentrates among specific constituencies (renters frustrated by housing policy, communities of color without responsive constituent service, younger adults concerned about economic opportunity) — understanding which and responding directly is the difference between maintaining 66% and allowing a credible challenger to emerge.",
          "Avoid the supermajority trap: a 66% result in a district with this much economic diversity, rental housing, and communities of color can erode quickly if the incumbent campaigns as though the margin is structural rather than maintained by active constituent engagement to renters, communities of color, and younger adults facing real economic constraints."
