          { name: "Generic Diversity Messaging as a Substitute for Specific Policy Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "MN-HD-44A's demographic diversity — 22.4% Asian, 7.67% Hispanic, 7.55% Black, plus ~7% mixed and other identities — is a strength, but treating this plurality as a unified voting bloc and substituting generic 'diversity and inclusion' messaging for specific policy responsiveness will activate skepticism rather than engagement. Each demographic community has distinct economic priorities, civic concerns, and policy expectations: Asian voters prioritize education and small-business support; Hispanic voters prioritize economic mobility and healthcare access; Black voters prioritize education equity and public safety. A representative who articulates specific policy commitments to each community's distinct priorities commands more credibility and durable support than one who treats diversity as a political category rather than a governance reality." }
