          { name: "Education Quality and Funding Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 68.9% college attainment and $148,810 median income, MN-HD-47B is a constituency of educated professionals with direct material stakes in K-12 school quality, higher education affordability, and workforce development. This is not a district where abstract education messaging works; voters evaluate schools on specific outcomes, curriculum rigor, and whether tax dollars are being deployed efficiently. A candidate who speaks with precision about education funding models, teacher retention, school accountability, and college affordability will command credibility with the dominant constituency cohort." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost Management for Affluent Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "Despite high income, the $148,810 median conceals real healthcare cost pressures for professional-class households—insurance premiums, out-of-pocket costs, and access to specialized care are material concerns. The 68.9% college attainment likely includes healthcare workers, insurance professionals, and others with specific knowledge of healthcare system dysfunction. A legislative agenda that addresses healthcare delivery efficiency, prescription drug costs, and access equity—rather than abstract healthcare expansion rhetoric—resonates with this constituency's actual experience and economic constraints." },
          { name: "Fiscal Sustainability and Tax Efficiency", tag: "careful", why: "The 46% Republican opposition in a high-income, highly educated district likely centers on concerns about tax burden, government efficiency, and whether public resources are being deployed effectively—not ideological opposition to government itself. These are substantive concerns held by persuadable swing voters who are not reflexively Republican but who evaluate on specific policy grounds. A Democratic incumbent who acknowledges the legitimacy of fiscal sustainability questions and offers concrete answers about how public resources improve outcomes will better position themselves to hold swing voters than one who dismisses these concerns as mere partisan talking points." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity Messaging Without Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 68.9% of the population is college-educated, partisan rhetoric without substantive policy grounding is actively counterproductive. This is a constituency that evaluates on specifics—education funding models, healthcare delivery mechanisms, environmental regulation, and tax policy are concrete governance questions, not abstract partisan positions. Any candidate who relies on partisan identity markers rather than detailed policy answers will find this affluent, educated electorate skeptical and persuadable by an alternative who offers substance." },
          { name: "Taking the 54% Margin for Granted Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "An 8-point margin in a district with 68.9% college attainment and $148,810 median income is meaningful but not a structural Democratic lock. The 46% Republican opposition is likely composed of sophisticated, affluent professionals who can be persuaded by a credible alternative offering on education, healthcare, fiscal management, or economic opportunity. An incumbent who campaigns on the assumption that the existing 54% is durable without active engagement on the specific governance priorities that moved those voters to vote Democratic risks finding those voters persuadable in a competitive cycle." }
