          "Address the renter and younger adult constituency explicitly rather than assuming they will simply support the Democratic incumbent: housing affordability, transit access, economic opportunity, and workplace protections are the specific governance concerns that activate younger, lower-asset households and renters in affluent districts—and these concerns can be addressed by a credible alternative if the incumbent is perceived as focused only on homeowner priorities.",
          "Protect against the specific vulnerability of fiscal conservatism messaging: in a high-income, high-tax district, a challenger framing government spending as inefficient and offering property tax reduction as a primary platform can appeal to homeowner anxieties about rising costs, even if that challenger is less credible on education or infrastructure—this risk is active and requires proactive messaging on spending efficiency and tax fairness.",
          "Recognize that a 65-35 Democratic margin is strong but not durable if governance competence is questioned: this constituency has the credential, income, and geographic mobility to switch parties or stay home if they conclude they are not receiving competent, results-driven governance—complacency based on the current margin is the single largest strategic error an incumbent can make in this district."
