          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Governance Outcomes", tag: "lean-into", why: "HD-64B's 59.8% college attainment rate and $84,451 median income identify a constituency that prioritizes education quality, public services, and neighborhood amenities as core governance deliverables. The 80-20 partisan result reflects confidence that Democratic governance delivers on these material outcomes; maintaining this alignment requires sustained legislative visibility on school funding, transit, and public safety — not partisan positioning. College-educated professionals in urban districts are more likely than other constituencies to reward specific policy wins and punish perceived neglect, making active constituent engagement on tangible outcomes essential to holding the 80% margin." },
          { name: "Renter Plurality & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 43.1% renter rate is unusually high for a district with $84,451 median income, indicating deliberate housing choices by younger professionals and downsizers who prioritize walkability and neighborhood quality over homeownership. This renter plurality is more mobile and more sensitive to housing costs and neighborhood outcomes than homeowners; they can shift political loyalty if a representative becomes invisible on housing affordability and neighborhood preservation. Direct engagement with renters' material concerns — zoning policy, property tax impacts on rents, transit access — is essential to maintaining the diverse coalition that anchors the 80% result." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity Without Active Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "Black residents comprise 10.81% of the district and Hispanic residents 4.55%, creating a diverse urban constituency whose economic mobility, education access, and healthcare concerns can become invisible in a safe seat if the incumbent defaults to college-educated professional concerns. While the partisan result suggests broad satisfaction with Democratic governance, specific attention to communities of color on police accountability, education equity, and economic opportunity prevents political drift and maintains the coalition's trust. Neglecting these communities' particular concerns in favor of generic Democratic messaging risks gradual disengagement." },
          { name: "Treating 80% as Permission to Disengage", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80-20 result is not a guarantee of durability — it is a reflection of the incumbent's alignment with the constituency's material expectations on schools, public safety, housing, and services. College-educated professionals and renters are more likely than other voters to withdraw support from representatives who become complacent or invisible; a shift toward a challenger perceived as more responsive to constituent priorities could erode the margin. The absence of competitive pressure is not an invitation to governance passivity but rather a requirement for sustained constituent service at a higher standard." },
          { name: "Assuming the Renter Plurality Has No Alternative", tag: "avoid", why: "Renters at 43.1% of the district are more mobile and more likely to shift political allegiance than homeowners, particularly if housing affordability worsens or neighborhood quality declines. An incumbent who takes renters for granted or defaults to homeowner-focused property-tax messaging risks alienating a significant portion of the constituent base that values urban walkability and affordability. Renters in safe-seat districts can organize political pressure or support for challengers if they perceive their concerns being systematically ignored by the incumbent." }
