          { name: "Treating the 100% Result as a Durable Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "Interpreting an unopposed 100% result as evidence of permanent Democratic dominance is analytically unsound and strategically dangerous. The district's young age profile, economic constraints, renter majority, and racial pluralism create multiple potential opposition entry points if the incumbent is perceived as failing on housing, neighborhood safety, or equitable service delivery. Any candidate who responds to 100% with complacency rather than investigation will be vulnerable to the next credible challenger who organizes around material governance failures." },
          { name: "Assuming Racial Demographic Consolidation Without Active Equity Work", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's balanced racial demographics—32.75% white, 30.91% Black, 19.38% Asian, 9.53% Hispanic—mean no group is inherently locked in, and each community's support is contingent on perceived responsive governance. An incumbent who assumes minority-community loyalty based on partisan affiliation rather than demonstrable investment in that community's economic opportunity, neighborhood safety, and equitable city services will find that loyalty vulnerable if a credible challenger invests in organizing within underserved communities. In a district this young and diverse, active equity engagement is not optional—it is the foundation of durable electoral support." }
